---
import Icon from './Icon.astro';
import RewardTeaching from './RewardTeaching.astro';
import { teachingExample as example, teachingScenarios } from '../data/mining-education.mjs';
const n = (value: number) => new Intl.NumberFormat('en-GB', { maximumFractionDigits: 3 }).format(value);
---
<div class="technical-resource mining-education">
  <nav class="technical-index lesson-index" aria-label="Lesson sections"><span class="technical-label">FOLLOW THE WORK</span><ul><li><a href="#relative-hashrate">01 · Your fraction of the work</a></li><li><a href="#difficulty-thresholds">02 · Three different thresholds</a></li><li><a href="#expected-versus-actual">03 · An average, not a schedule</a></li><li><a href="#model-assumptions">Model assumptions & limits</a></li></ul></nav>
  <RewardTeaching />
  <section class="lesson-section" aria-labelledby="relative-hashrate">
    <span class="technical-label">01 / RATE → RELATIVE WORK</span><h2 id="relative-hashrate" tabindex="-1">Your fraction of the work</h2>
    <p>A mining hash is one proof-of-work result computed from candidate block data. The miner varies the work and tests results against a target. Hashrate measures this computation: <strong>1 H/s means one hash per second</strong>. More attempts increase expected discoveries; they do not reserve a winning result.</p>
    <figure class="rate-figure" aria-labelledby="rate-caption">
      <div class="rate-nodes"><div class="miner-node"><Icon name="cpu" size="diagram" /><span>ONE MINER</span><strong>{n(example.minerHashrate)} <small>H/s</small></strong><p>h · constant teaching rate</p></div><div class="rate-connector" aria-hidden="true">contributes to <b>→</b></div><div class="network-node"><Icon name="network" size="diagram" /><span>WHOLE NETWORK</span><strong>{n(example.networkHashrate)} <small>H/s</small></strong><p>H · includes this miner</p></div></div>
      <div class="rate-ratio"><span>RELATIVE EXPECTED CONTRIBUTION</span><strong>1,000 ÷ 1,000,000 = 0.001 = 0.1%</strong><div class="rate-strip" aria-hidden="true"><span style="width:0.1%"></span></div><p>One part in 1,000. This strip is proportional: the orange fraction may be narrower than a screen pixel. Read the exact percentage above.</p></div>
      <figcaption id="rate-caption">Hypothetical rates, not observed network data. At 1,000 H/s out of 1,000,000 H/s (1 MH/s), this miner contributes 0.1% of the network’s hashing work under constant conditions.</figcaption>
    </figure>
    <div class="protocol-note"><strong>Protocol rule</strong><p>Monero targets a <strong>120-second (two-minute)</strong> block interval and adjusts difficulty over time. Actual intervals vary. <a href="https://docs.getmonero.org/technical-specs/#block-time" rel="external noreferrer">Monero technical specification</a>.</p></div>
    <p><strong>Network difficulty</strong> is the dimensionless work parameter used to judge a block’s proof of work. In this idealized model, difficulty D is the expected number of hashes per block. A harder target requires more work on average; no individual attempt accumulates credit toward a guaranteed block.</p>
    <div class="formula-note"><span class="technical-label">MODEL / CONSTANT CONDITIONS</span><p>H ≈ D ÷ T &nbsp; · &nbsp; D ≈ H × T</p><p>1,000,000 H/s × 120 s = <strong>120,000,000</strong> difficulty</p></div>
    <p>That conversion uses the target interval T, not an observed guarantee. Published network hashrate is inferred from difficulty and block timing over a window; XMRig’s local speed is a different measurement. This page retrieves neither. For a trustworthy local rate, <a href="/troubleshoot/xmrig-low-hashrate/#keep-a-small-measurement-record">record comparable conditions and averaging windows</a>.</p>
  </section>
  <section class="lesson-section" aria-labelledby="difficulty-thresholds">
    <span class="technical-label">02 / SAME WORK → DIFFERENT TESTS</span><h2 id="difficulty-thresholds" tabindex="-1">Three thresholds, three meanings</h2>
    <p>A job can ask for submissions easier to find than a Monero block. The <code>diff</code> in an XMRig job message is the job’s difficulty; do not copy it into a calculator’s network-difficulty field.</p>
    <figure class="threshold-figure" aria-labelledby="threshold-caption">
      <div class="threshold-origin"><Icon name="flowArrow" size="diagram" /><strong>One stream of computed hashes</strong><span>Each result is tested against the applicable target.</span></div>
      <ol class="threshold-list"><li><span class="threshold-id">A</span><div><h3>Job / submission target</h3><p>Work an endpoint asks your miner to submit. Acceptance records that endpoint’s response.</p><span class="technical-label">ENDPOINT POLICY</span></div></li><li><span class="threshold-id">B</span><div><h3>P2Pool sidechain target</h3><p>A qualifying sidechain share has a separate threshold. A lower-difficulty accepted submission need not qualify.</p><span class="technical-label">P2POOL ONLY</span></div></li><li><span class="threshold-id">C</span><div><h3>Monero network target</h3><p>A valid Monero block must meet the network’s proof-of-work threshold and other consensus rules.</p><span class="technical-label">MONERO CONSENSUS</span></div></li></ol>
      <figcaption id="threshold-caption">Conceptual branches, not a numerical scale or three mandatory stages. Conventional pools do not have a P2Pool sidechain stage. Difficulty is inversely related to the numeric hash target: higher difficulty means a harder test.</figcaption>
    </figure>
    <p><strong>Pool policy:</strong> conventional pools choose their own share targets, accounting method, fees and payment threshold. An accepted share is evidence of work accepted there; its payment treatment depends on those rules.</p>
    <p><strong>P2Pool:</strong> payout eligibility depends on qualifying sidechain work in its PPLNS window when P2Pool finds a Monero block. XMRig’s accepted counter alone cannot establish this. <a href="https://github.com/SChernykh/p2pool#how-payouts-work-in-p2pool" rel="external noreferrer">P2Pool’s payout explanation</a> and <a href="/troubleshoot/xmrig-pool-shares-payments/#p2pool-shares">Ember’s P2Pool evidence checklist</a> explain the distinction.</p>
    <p>Algorithm names in mining software describe supported or assigned work. Support for an algorithm does not establish its activation on the Monero network. Inspect the <a href="/troubleshoot/xmrig-log-reference/#new-job">source-scoped job message</a> alongside the intended endpoint and network documentation.</p>
  </section>
  <section class="lesson-section" aria-labelledby="expected-versus-actual">
    <span class="technical-label">03 / EXPECTED WORK → EXPECTED REWARD</span><h2 id="expected-versus-actual" tabindex="-1">An average is not a payment schedule</h2>
    <p>Keep the same hypothetical rate and difficulty. In one hour, 1,000 H/s × 3,600 s = <strong>3,600,000 hashes</strong>. Dividing by difficulty gives <strong>0.03 expected block equivalents</strong>.</p>
    <figure class="expectation-figure" aria-labelledby="expectation-caption">
      <div class="expected-calculation"><span class="technical-label">IDEALIZED EXPECTED VALUE / 1 HOUR</span><div><strong>0.03</strong><span>expected block equivalents</span></div><b aria-hidden="true">×</b><div><strong>0.5 XMR</strong><span>invented reward per block</span></div><b aria-hidden="true">=</b><div class="expected-total"><strong>0.015 XMR</strong><span>expected gross reward</span></div></div>
      <div class="actual-outcomes"><span class="technical-label">TWO CONDITIONAL SOLO OUTCOMES / SAME HOUR</span><div><p><strong>If no block is found</strong><span>0 blocks × assumed reward</span></p><strong>0 XMR</strong></div><div><p><strong>If one block is found</strong><span>1 block × assumed reward</span></p><strong>0.5 XMR</strong></div></div>
      <figcaption id="expectation-caption">Deterministic arithmetic possibilities, not simulated or observed runs. These are two illustrative outcomes, not an exhaustive distribution; no probabilities are assigned. The expected 0.03 block equivalents is not a fraction of a discovered block or a balance owed.</figcaption>
    </figure>
    <p><strong>The 0.5 XMR reward is invented solely for teaching.</strong> It is not a current base reward or a claim about every block. Real total rewards depend on emission, transaction fees and any applicable block-size penalty. No fee, stale work, downtime or cost is deducted here.</p>
    <div class="technical-table" role="region" aria-label="Constant-assumption teaching arithmetic" tabindex="0"><table><caption>More time scales expectation under the same hypothetical inputs</caption><thead><tr><th scope="col">Active time</th><th scope="col">Hashes performed</th><th scope="col">Expected block equivalents</th><th scope="col">Expected gross XMR</th></tr></thead><tbody>{teachingScenarios.map((s) => <tr><th scope="row">{s.hours} hour{s.hours === 1 ? '' : 's'}</th><td>{n(s.hashes)}</td><td>{n(s.expectedBlocks)}</td><td>{n(s.grossXmr)}</td></tr>)}</tbody></table></div>
    <p>Doubling hashrate doubles modeled expectation at fixed difficulty and runtime. It does not guarantee a block or payment by a deadline. Even a model’s average waiting time is not a countdown. Pool and P2Pool payments require separate accounting and eligibility evidence.</p>
    <p>To enter your own assumptions, use the <a href="/tools/monero-mining-profitability-calculator/#network-assumptions">mining profitability calculator</a>. It applies the same expected-work arithmetic, then models pool fee, price and electricity; it does not predict the next block or a pool’s payment schedule.</p>
  </section>
</div>
